<InfoPanel Title="The Business Problem" Subtitle="Why this kind of system matters.">
    <ChildContent>
        <p>
            In small and medium businesses, important follow-up work often starts in informal places:
            an email, a Teams message, a phone call, a spreadsheet row, a paper form, a maintenance request,
            a training gap, a document review, or a safety conversation. Without a workflow layer, work can be
            missed, repeated, delayed, or completed without evidence.
        </p>
    </ChildContent>
</InfoPanel>

<InfoPanel Title="What OperationsFlow Solves" Subtitle="The business value shown by the prototype.">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="of-review-proof-grid">
            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Capture</strong>
                <span>Turns scattered follow-up work into records with title, owner, due date, priority, status, notes, and module context.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Accountability</strong>
                <span>Shows who owns the work, what is overdue, what is high priority, and what needs review first.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Evidence</strong>
                <span>Lets records carry supporting files such as PDFs, photos, forms, scans, notes, and source documents.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Traceability</strong>
                <span>Activity Log and record history help prove what was created, updated, uploaded, deleted, or reviewed.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Management Review</strong>
                <span>Reports, workload, reminders, and data quality views turn operational data into review actions.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Production Direction</strong>
                <span>The local proof is shaped so Microsoft Lists, SharePoint, Power Automate, and Power BI-style reporting can be added later.</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
</InfoPanel>

