<InfoPanel Title="Why The Evidence Layer Matters" Subtitle="This is the difference between a dashboard and a workflow system.">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="of-review-proof-grid">
            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Before</strong>
                <span>A dashboard can say something is overdue, but it may not prove what happened or what evidence exists.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>After</strong>
                <span>OperationsFlow can show the record, the owner, the due date, the attached evidence, and the activity log.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Management Review</strong>
                <span>Reports and Data Quality can now flag records that should have files but do not.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Audit Mindset</strong>
                <span>Even as a prototype, the app shows the kind of thinking needed for reviewability and handover.</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
</InfoPanel>

<InfoPanel Title="Portfolio Value" Subtitle="What this project says about the developer.">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="of-review-proof-grid">
            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Business Analysis</strong>
                <span>Identifies a real operational pain point and turns it into a workflow model.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Full-Stack Thinking</strong>
                <span>Combines data models, UI pages, services, storage, reports, exports, and activity history.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Reusable Components</strong>
                <span>Uses shared cards, filters, tables, detail layouts, and attachment panels across modules.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Production Awareness</strong>
                <span>Separates prototype proof from production needs like authentication, permissions, SharePoint, retention, and deployment.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Microsoft 365 Direction</strong>
                <span>Maps naturally to Microsoft Lists, SharePoint document libraries, Power Automate, Teams/Outlook, and Power BI-style review.</span>
            </div>

            <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                <strong>Real-World Relevance</strong>
                <span>Matches the kind of operational automation and workflow thinking used in admin, safety, engineering, and business systems work.</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
</InfoPanel>

<InfoPanel Title="Prototype Boundaries" Subtitle="Honest limits before calling this production-ready.">
    <ChildContent>
        <ul>
            <li>The current app uses local SQLite demo data.</li>
            <li>The current document library uses local storage, not SharePoint production storage.</li>
            <li>Authentication and role-based permissions are not fully implemented yet.</li>
            <li>Production audit retention, backups, and operational ownership still need to be defined.</li>
            <li>Microsoft Lists, SharePoint, Teams, Outlook, Power Automate, and Power BI integration are Week 4 direction items.</li>
            <li>The project does not replace a full ERP, job management system, or safety platform; it proves a workflow layer and integration direction.</li>
        </ul>
    </ChildContent>
</InfoPanel>

<GuidanceNote Title="Business Value Summary" Icon="💡" Tone="info">
    <ul>
        <li>OperationsFlow is strongest as proof of practical business-system thinking.</li>
        <li>The project connects workflow capture, evidence, management reporting, data quality, and traceability.</li>
        <li>The evidence layer makes it stronger than a basic CRUD dashboard.</li>
        <li>Week 4 should focus on proving the production path with Microsoft 365, SharePoint, authentication, and deployment readiness.</li>
    </ul>
</GuidanceNote>